Tests for the patches in patches/firefox/, run by hand and in GitLab CI, see .gitlab-ci.yml. Firefox's own tests
do not run in the package build.

Both drive Firefox through Marionette, with a new profile in a directory of their own and on a port of their
own, so they run next to your own Firefox without touching it. Pass the Firefox to test, usually the installed
/usr/lib/firefox/firefox, or one extracted from a package with dpkg-deb -x.

Session restore: run-session.sh and driver.py
    Starts a headless mutter with one virtual monitor in a D-Bus session of its own and runs Firefox three
    times against it: the first run opens three windows and quits, the second starts again and restores that
    session, and the third restores the session the second one saved. It covers the fixes of the session
    patches and mutter's side of the protocol, but not whether windows return to their workspaces, which needs
    a desktop.

    Only the mutter package has mutter's binary. Instead of installing it, extract it with its library from the
    local repository, or from any build to test:

      mkdir /tmp/mutter-root
      for p in mutter libmutter-18-0; do dpkg-deb -x /usr/local/lib/debs/${p}_*.deb /tmp/mutter-root; done
      ./run-session.sh /tmp/mutter-root - /usr/lib/firefox/firefox ubuntu:GNOME /tmp/session-test menu-restore

    The second argument is "-" for the installed GTK, or a directory libgtk-3-0t64 was extracted into. The
    fourth is XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP: ubuntu:GNOME is what Firefox sees on this desktop, and an unpatched Firefox
    disables session restore for it, while any value without GNOME, like test, gets past that check. The last
    argument is the scenario, described at the top of driver.py:

      menu-restore     the second run starts without restoring and restores from the History menu; Firefox
                       159 crashes with "Name of toplevel is already in use" when the check is passed
      startup-restore  the second run restores at startup and opens a new window with the start page beside
                       the restored ones
      startup-no-ids   like startup-restore, from a profile saved without IDs; Firefox 159 shows none of
                       its windows when the check is passed
      startup-url      like startup-restore, and the second run starts with a URL on the command line while
                       external links open in new windows, so the new window shows the URL; Firefox 159 opens it
                       as a tab of the first restored window
      startup-url-tab  like startup-url, with external links opening in tabs, as by default; the URL opens as a
                       tab of the first restored window, with and without the patches

    Each window of the first run shows a page of its own, and the second run starts with an activation token in
    DESKTOP_STARTUP_ID and XDG_ACTIVATION_TOKEN, as a launcher passes it. With the patches, the first run reports 3
    windows, the second 4, or 3 in menu-restore and startup-url-tab, which open no new window, and the third run
    restores the windows the second one saved and opens a new window beside them. The new window of startup-restore
    and startup-no-ids shows about:blank, the start page of the test, which the session store does not save, so
    their third run has 3 restored windows and 4 in all, and that of startup-url 4 and 5. Before the third run, the
    test gives the last window of the saved session the ID {12345678-1234-4123-8123-123456789abc}, whose first group
    consists of decimal digits, as about one in 43 random IDs does, and Firefox has to restore that window under it
    ("restore"), which without wayland-session-restore-uuid-ids.patch it adds under a new ID instead. The compositor
    does not know that ID, so it restores every other window the second run saved. The second run of every startup
    scenario reports the pages of the tabs of each window and which windows the activation token is passed with: the
    new window, which the compositor adds under a new ID ("add"), never a restored one ("restore"), and none in
    startup-url-tab. startup-restore and startup-url also report the window with the focus, the new one. In
    menu-restore the token is passed with the window Firefox starts with. Each run exits with status 0. The script
    then prints "RESULT: OK" and exits with 0. A value which differs is marked with FAIL, and the script exits with 1.
    With ubuntu:GNOME, an unpatched Firefox restores its windows without session management, so the checks of
    windows restored by the compositor and of the window given the ID above fail, and without
    session-restore-launch-window.patch and wayland-launch-activation-token.patch also those of the new window and
    the token. The session requests are in the WAYLAND_DEBUG output of each run in the output directory:

      grep -h -o -E '(add|restore)_toplevel\([^)]*"[^"]*"|toplevel_session_v1#[0-9]+\.restored\(\)|activate\("[^"]*", wl_surface#[0-9]+\)' /tmp/session-test/run*.log

    The second run of menu-restore and startup-no-ids adds its windows under new IDs, the second run of the other
    startup scenarios restores the windows of the first and adds the new one, and the third run restores exactly
    the windows the second one saved, each answered with restored() except the one given the ID above. The D-Bus
    and portal messages printed on the way are expected in the private session.

Wasm sandboxed libraries: test_wasm.py
    Runs Firefox headless and makes each of the six libraries do some work: expat parses XML, hunspell checks
    spelling, ogg decodes and soundtouch plays a short tone ffmpeg generates, woff2 loads a WOFF2 font, and
    graphite lays out text in a Graphite font. Needs ffmpeg and fonts-sil-andika, which has both fonts.

      python3 -I test_wasm.py /usr/lib/firefox/firefox /tmp/wasm-test

    Every library should report a result and none an error:

      expat      root, parsererror elements: 0
      hunspell   en-US: hello=true helo=false
      ogg        decoded 2.00 s
      woff2      loaded
      graphite   loaded, laid out width 459.9
      soundtouch played to 2.00 s at rate 1.5
      still running afterwards: True
      exit status 0
    RESULT: OK

    The script exits with 0 then, and with 1 when a library reported an error or Firefox did not keep running or
    did not exit with status 0.

    The test shows that the libraries work, not that they run sandboxed. That they are built sandboxed is
    shown by the check in ../README, which lists the rlbox_wasm2c_ symbols in libxul.so and liblgpllibs.so.
